From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: SplitPlay <splitplay@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 13:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] splitplay: scheduler protection (no EcoQoS, MMCSS for the hot
 threads)

Several desks share one PC with the games they stream. A worker is a
windowless process in a non-console RDP session, which Windows 11 treats
as background work: it may get EcoQoS (efficiency cores, lower clocks)
and its timer-resolution requests may be ignored. Its streaming threads
run at priority 13-15 (HIGH class while streaming), which ties with a
game's TIME_CRITICAL thread (15 in the normal class), so they share
quanta round-robin with N games.

New module src/splitplay/scheduling.{h,cpp}:

- apply_process_policy(), called from main() right after logging starts:
  SetProcessInformation(ProcessPowerThrottling) with ControlMask
  EXECUTION_SPEED | IGNORE_TIMER_RESOLUTION and StateMask 0, i.e. never
  throttle execution speed and always honour timer-resolution requests
  (Windows 10, which does not know the second flag, gets the first one
  alone). Then the priority class ABOVE_NORMAL.
- Priority class: upstream already switches to HIGH while a stream runs
  (streaming_will_start) and back to NORMAL after it; streaming_will_stop
  now returns to ABOVE_NORMAL. HIGH is kept for streaming because the
  threads that are not under MMCSS (audio send, RTSP, heartbeat) then
  still run above every normal-class game thread but TIME_CRITICAL;
  ABOVE_NORMAL when idle, because an idle desk does almost nothing, yet
  its control pipe heartbeat (3 misses = restart) and the next stream's
  start-up must not queue behind other desks' games. REALTIME is never
  used.
- mmcss_t: AvSetMmThreadCharacteristicsW("Games") +
  AvSetMmThreadPriority(AVRT_PRIORITY_HIGH) for the lifetime of the
  object. "Games" rather than "Pro Audio": its default scheduling
  category Medium (16-22) is above every normal-class game thread, but
  below the High category (23-26) of "Audio"/"Pro Audio", which the
  session's audio engine and Sunshine's own audio capture (audio.cpp) use,
  so video never takes the CPU from audio. MMCSS keeps its reserve for
  other threads (SystemResponsiveness, 20 % by default), so a busy worker
  cannot starve the PC.
  Registered: video::capture, the encode thread (capture_async),
  video::capture_sync (encoders without parallel encoding),
  stream::videoBroadcast, stream::controlBroadcast (receives and decrypts
  input) and, on its first input message, the task-pool thread that
  injects input (join_mmcss_for_this_thread, reverted when the thread
  ends).

High-resolution timers: the two pacing sleeps (capture slots in
display_base.cpp, send pacing in stream.cpp) already use upstream's
high_precision_timer (CREATE_WAITABLE_TIMER_HIGH_RESOLUTION), and
streaming_will_start already requests the 0.5 ms timer resolution
(NtSetTimerResolution, timeBeginPeriod(1) as fallback). Nothing else in
the hot path sleeps for pacing, so this patch only makes the request
effective with the IGNORE_TIMER_RESOLUTION opt-out.

Everything applied is logged ("SplitPlay: scheduling: ..." once, and one
"runs under MMCSS" line per thread); a refusal is a warning and the
thread keeps its upstream priority.

